      "side": 1,
      "message": "Note that this code could be a lot simpler and less error prone if written in C++. Since I had to make so many changes to the current code and it has taken so long for you to respond to feedback, let\u0027s get the C version merged first.\n\nThen you can use create a followup CL to convert to C++23, using this as a guide:\n\n#### 1. Replace `goto` Cleanup with RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization)\n**The Benefit:** Guaranteed cleanup of memory, file handles, and USB contexts even if an error occurs and the function returns early. You will never have a memory leak or dangling file descriptor again.\n**How to do it:**\n* **File I/O:** Stop using `FILE*`, `fopen`, and `fclose`. Use `std::ifstream`. The file is automatically closed when the stream goes out of scope.\n* **Memory:** Replace `malloc()` and `free()` with `std::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e`.\n* **libusb Handles:** Wrap `libusb_device_handle*` and `libusb_context*` in `std::unique_ptr` with custom deleters.\n\n**Before (C-style):**\n```c\nFILE *file \u003d fopen(name, \"rb\");\nunsigned char *buffer \u003d malloc(len);\nif (!buffer) goto exit;\n// ... error happens ...\nret \u003d -1;\ngoto exit;\n\nexit:\nif (file) fclose(file);\nif (buffer) free(buffer);\nreturn ret;\n```\n\n**After (C++23):**\n```cpp\n// Look how clean this is! No goto, no manual cleanup.\nstd::ifstream file(name, std::ios::binary);\nif (!file) return std::unexpected(Error::FileOpenFailed);\n\nstd::vector\u003cuint8_t\u003e buffer(len, 0xFF);\n// If an error happens, just return. \u0027buffer\u0027 and \u0027file\u0027 clean themselves up.\n```\n\n#### 2. Ditch Raw Pointers for `std::span` and `std::array`\n**The Benefit:** Raw pointers (`uint8_t* buf, int len`) lose their size information, leading to out-of-bounds reads/writes. `std::span` binds the pointer and its size together safely without copying data.\n**How to do it:**\n* For fixed-size command buffers, use `std::array\u003cuint8_t, 16\u003e` instead of `unsigned char cmd[16]`.\n* When passing buffers to functions, take `std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e` (mutable) or `std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e` (read-only).\n\n**Example:**\n```cpp\n// C style\nint mbedtls_usb_send(const uint8_t *buf, uint32_t len);\n\n// C++23 style\nauto mbedtls_usb_send(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e data) -\u003e std::expected\u003csize_t, UsbError\u003e;\n```\n\n#### 3. Modern Error Handling with `std::expected`\n**The Benefit:** Currently, functions return `-1` or print logs to handle errors. C++23\u0027s `std::expected` explicitly states that a function returns *either* a valid value *or* an error code, forcing the caller to handle failures safely.\n**How to do it:**\n```cpp\nenum class FlashError { Success, UsbFailed, FileNotFound, VerifyFailed };\n\n// Returns either the firmware version string, or an error.\nauto Get_boot_version() -\u003e std::expected\u003cstd::string, FlashError\u003e {\n    std::array\u003cuint8_t, 36\u003e databuffer{};\n    // ... usb transfer ...\n    if (transfer_failed) return std::unexpected(FlashError::UsbFailed);\n    \n    return std::string(databuffer.begin(), databuffer.end());\n}\n```\n\n#### 4. Safer Strings, Paths, and Formatting\n**The Benefit:** Fixes buffer overflow vulnerabilities natively and makes string manipulation trivial.\n**How to do it:**\n* **Strings:** Replace `char tmp[PATH_MAX]` and `strcpy` with `std::string` or `std::string_view`.\n* **Paths:** Replace manual `fseek` length calculations with C++ filesystem utilities.\n* **Formatting:** Replace `printf` and `sprintf` with C++23 `std::print` and `std::format`.\n\n**Before (C-style):**\n```c\n// Prone to overflow, manual sizing\nchar time_buff[64];\nsprintf(time_buff, \"%s.%03ld\", buff, tv.tv_usec / 1000);\n```\n\n**After (C++23):**\n```cpp\n// Type-safe, memory-safe, no format string vulnerabilities\nauto time_str \u003d std::format(\"{}.{:03}\", buff, tv.tv_usec / 1000);\n```\n\n#### 5. Endianness and Bit Manipulation\n**The Benefit:** Eliminates messy macros like `htole32` or the custom `U32_SET` macro in favor of safe, compiler-checked standard library calls.\n**How to do it:**\nUse C++23\u0027s `\u003cbit\u003e` header. You can use `std::byteswap` and `std::endian::native` to safely handle byte-order conversions directly in the command arrays.",
